s=s.split('## Proof')[0]+r'''## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 **The approved integral map.** Identify $C_{\mathrm M}(\mu)^\theta$ with $\theta(M(\mu))/\theta(K_\mu)$, where $K_\mu=M(\mu)\cap I^{\rhd\mu}$. By [F2], literal left multiplication by $q^{-N}x_\lambda T_{w_\lambda}$ kills $\theta(K_\mu)$ and induces the surjection $F:C_{\mathrm M}(\mu)^\theta\to P(\lambda)$ sending the class of $x_\mu$ to $z_\lambda$. In particular $F(m)=q^{-N}x_\lambda T_{w_\lambda}\theta(\widetilde m)$ for any row-module lift, independently of that lift. Both modules are free of rank $f^\mu=f^\lambda$; finite basis preimages give a right inverse of the square matrix of $F$, so its determinant is a unit and $F$ is an isomorphism. Set $\Phi_\lambda=F^{-1}$. Its $H$-linearity with the twisted target action gives $\Phi_\lambda(z_\lambda h)=m_0\theta(h)$, where this expression uses the ordinary action on the underlying Murphy module. All these matrices and their inverses lie in the universal Laurent ring and specialize by [F8]. [F1, F2, F4, F8, algebra]

2.1 **Generic uniqueness of the forms, after the integral isomorphism.** Work over $A=\mathbb Z[v^{\pm1}]$ with formal $q=v$ and $K=\mathbb Q(v)$. The matrix of the regular trace form $(a,b)\mapsto\operatorname{Tr}_K(L_{ab})$ has Laurent-polynomial entries. At $v=1$ its matrix is $n!$ times the permutation matrix $u\leftrightarrow u^{-1}$: in the regular group basis left multiplication by a nonidentity group element has trace zero, whereas the identity has trace $n!$. Its determinant is therefore nonzero, so the regular trace form is nonsingular over $K$. If $a\in J(H_K)$, every $ab$ is nilpotent by [F6], and $L_{ab}$ has trace zero; nondegeneracy forces $a=0$, so $H_K$ is semisimple. Each cellular layer is an ideal in a quotient of this semisimple algebra, with multiplication $(a\otimes b)(c\otimes d)=G_\mu(b,c)a\otimes d$. If $G_\mu$ had a nonzero radical, $(\operatorname{rad}G_\mu)\otimes C_{\mathrm M}(\mu)$ would be a nonzero square-zero ideal of that quotient, stable under its surrounding algebra by the cell law and contravariance, a contradiction. Thus $G_\mu$ is nonsingular, and the cellular rank-one operators realize the full matrix algebra on $C_{\mathrm M}(\mu)_K$. Every module endomorphism is scalar because it commutes with all matrix units; this conclusion also holds on the $\theta$-twist, since $\theta(H_K)=H_K$. On that twist, any contravariant form $B'$ defines an operator $U$ by $G_\mu^\theta(Ux,y)=B'(x,y)$. Contravariance and nonsingularity imply that $U$ commutes with the module action, so $B'$ is a scalar multiple of $G_\mu^\theta$. No generic argument was used to establish the integral map or basis. [F1, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 **The exact generator computation.** The expansion in [F2] has distinct distinguished row-module vectors and length $L+\ell(u)$, so [F3] gives
$$\beta(z_\lambda,z_\lambda)=\sum_{u\in S_\mu}q^{-2\ell(u)}q^{L+\ell(u)}=q^L\sum_{u\in S_\mu}q^{-\ell(u)}=q^{L-N}P_\mu(q).$$
The last equality pairs $u$ with $w_0(S_\mu)u$. By [F4], $G_\mu(m_0,m_0)=P_\mu(q)$. Therefore, with the specified normalization $\Phi_\lambda(z_\lambda)=m_0$, the only possible comparison unit is $u_\lambda(q)=q^{N-L}$. This computation uses the distinguished-basis definition of $\beta$, not any incorrectly unscaled trace identity. [F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra]

4.1 **Universal form identity.** Transfer the DJ form to $C_{\mathrm M}(\mu)^\theta$ using $F=\Phi_\lambda^{-1}$: set $B'(c,d)=\beta(Fc,Fd)$. This is contravariant for the twisted action, as is $G_\mu^\theta$. Both are nonzero over $K$, since their respective generator values are $q^{L-N}P_\mu(q)$ and $P_\mu(q)$, nonzero in that field. Step 2.1 makes them proportional, and step 3.1 determines the scalar as $q^{N-L}$, proving $G_\mu^\theta(\Phi_\lambda x,\Phi_\lambda y)=q^{N-L}\beta(x,y)$ over $K$. Both sides have matrices in $A$; the injection $A\to K$ proves their equality already in $A$, and then [F8] specializes it to every commutative $R$ with unit parameter. No cancellation of $P_\mu(q)$ takes place after specialization. [F3, F4, F5, F8, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 **Radicals, divisibility, quotients and duality.** The unit form identity and the isomorphism $\Phi_\lambda$ identify the form radicals and yield $D^{\lambda}_{\mathrm{DJ}}\cong(D^{\lambda'}_{\mathrm M})^\theta$, including zero quotients. Over a field, a vector outside a nonzero cell-form radical generates its whole cell module: choose a vector pairing to $1$ and use its finite basis expansion in the cell rank-one action $x m_{uv}=G_\mu(x,m_u)m_v$. Thus every proper submodule lies in that radical, making it the unique maximal submodule and the nonzero form quotient the simple head; transfer this conclusion through $\Phi_\lambda$. If the form is identically zero only the zero form quotient is asserted, not a zero actual head. Over a PID with chosen prime $\pi$, the submodules $\{x:\beta(x,P(\lambda))\subseteq\pi^iR\}$ for every $i\ge0$ are identified with their Murphy counterparts because the forms differ by a unit; their reductions correspond as well. Applying step 1.1 to $\lambda'$ gives $P(\lambda')\cong C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda)^\theta$. Compose with the same-shape perfect pairing [F7] and the commuting of star dual with $\theta$ in [F1] to obtain $P(\lambda')\cong P(\lambda)^{\circ,\theta}$. Empty shapes have $N=L=0$, $\Phi=1$ and unit $1$. This proves all clauses, using generic semisimplicity only for uniqueness of forms on an already integral module. No choice principle is used. [F1, F4, F7, F8, step 1.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
'''
