s=s.split('## Proof')[0]+r'''## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 **The group algebra over every coefficient ring.** Let $R$ be any commutative ring and specialize at $q=1$. The Hecke relations become $T_i^2=1$, braid and distant commutation. The Coxeter presentation in [F5] therefore gives the homomorphism $H_{R,1}(S_n)\to R[S_n]$ carrying $T_i$ to $s_i$. A reduced word shows that it carries every $T_w$ to $w$. These are bases of the source and target over $R$, so the map is an isomorphism over $R$, including in positive characteristic and with zero divisors. Equivalently the assignment $s_i\mapsto T_i$ gives its inverse directly from the presentation. No complex-field specialization, dimension comparison over a field or semisimplicity is used. [F5, algebra]

1.2 **The actual polytabloids and the forms.** Give the classical tabloid module the right action $\{t\}\cdot w=\{w^{-1}\cdot t\}=\{t\cdot w\}$. The map $b_d=x_\lambda T_d\mapsto\{t^\lambda\cdot d\}$ sends the distinguished basis to the tabloid basis. It respects the right action: at $q=1$ the row-module multiplication rule is simply the permutation of these cosets, with a same-row transposition acting as $1$. Every diagonal value of $\beta$ becomes $1$, so this is an isometry to the orthonormal tabloid form. The column group of the column-filled tableau $t_\lambda$ is exactly $S_{\lambda'}$, because its columns carry the consecutive row blocks of $\lambda'$. Hence $z_\lambda=x_\lambda w_\lambda\sum_{u\in S_{\lambda'}}\operatorname{sgn}(u)u$ maps to $\sum_u\operatorname{sgn}(u)\{t_\lambda\cdot u\}$, the classical column polytabloid of $t_\lambda$ (replace $u$ by $u^{-1}$ to compare with the left-action formula). Covariance shows $e_t=z_\lambda d_{\lambda'}(t)$ maps to the polytabloid of $t_\lambda\cdot d_{\lambda'}(t)=t'$, the transpose of the standard $\lambda'$-tableau $t$. Thus the actual standard bases correspond bijectively. By the split integral classical basis in [F3] and the split Hecke basis in [F5], this identifies $P(\lambda)$ with $R\otimes_{\mathbb Z}S^\lambda_{\mathbb Z}$ over every $R$, and the restricted DJ form with the restricted tabloid form. [F3, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 **The exact position of the twist.** At $q=1$, [F2] gives $\theta(T_i)=-T_i$, hence $\theta(w)=\operatorname{sgn}(w)w$. Therefore the right module $P(\lambda)^\theta$ is the sign tensor twist. Applying the involution $\theta$ to [F1] yields
$$P(\lambda)^\theta\cong C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda'),$$
without a remaining twist on the Murphy cell. Combining this with the independent same-shape pairing in [F6] gives $P(\lambda)^\theta\cong P(\lambda')^\circ$. Under step 1.2, star dual is the ordinary group contragredient dual, so this is exactly the module identity $S_F^\lambda\otimes\operatorname{sgn}\cong(S_F^{\lambda'})^*$ of [F4] after converting the published left modules to right modules. This asserts the same compatible module dictionary, not an unproved equality of separately normalized explicit maps. In characteristic $2$ the sign becomes trivial at $q=1$; conjugation remains part of the identity. [F1, F2, F4, F6, step 1.2, algebra]

3.1 **Multiplicities and zero quotients.** For a field and unit $q$, the unit form identity in [F1] identifies $D^{\mu}_{\mathrm{DJ}}$ with $(D^{\mu'}_{\mathrm M})^\theta$, including zero quotients. A nonzero cell-form quotient is simple: the cell rank-one action shows that every vector outside its radical generates the cell module, so the radical is its unique maximal submodule. Thus when $D^{\mu'}_{\mathrm M}\ne0$, the module $D^{\mu}_{\mathrm{DJ}}$ is a legitimate simple composition-factor label. Applying the exact twist equivalence in [F2] to a composition series and using [F1] gives
$$[P(\lambda):D^{\mu}_{\mathrm{DJ}}]=[C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda')^\theta:D^{\mu}_{\mathrm{DJ}}]=[C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda'):(D^{\mu}_{\mathrm{DJ}})^\theta]=[C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda'):D^{\mu'}_{\mathrm M}].$$
This proves clause 2. If a form is identically zero, only its zero form quotient is asserted; no claim is made that the actual module head vanishes. [F1, F2, F6, algebra]

4.1 **Conditional Schur dictionary and conclusion.** Under the stated separate hypothesis on a Schur corner, its images at $\lambda'$ are $C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda')$ and $D^{\lambda'}_{\mathrm M}$. The untwisted Murphy identities from step 2.1 and [F1], valid for arbitrary unit $q$ by involutivity of $\theta$, identify these with $P(\lambda)^\theta$ and $(D^{\lambda}_{\mathrm{DJ}})^\theta$, including the zero quotient. This proves clause 3 without a Schur theorem as a prerequisite. The actual nonzero Schur head and the Hecke cell-form quotient are distinct notions when the corner kills the former. For the empty shape, the generators, forms, twists and all isomorphisms have value $1$. All bases, maps and scalar identities are explicit and use no choice principle. [F1, F2, F6, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
'''
